            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to struggle, then the Mandarin for with one's own hands."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: shǎndiàn",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shǎndiàn — \"lightning\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ zuì pà dǎléi hé shǎndiàn — Thunder and lightning are what I'm most afraid of."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shǎndiàn is built from shǎn and diàn 'lightning', both inherited from Middle Chinese, syemX and denH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary glosses the first as the look of something coming out of a door, and explains the second as the flash when yin and yang clash; the sense 'to flash' is not given for the first."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
