          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rónghuà is built from róng and huà 'to change', both inherited from Middle Chinese, yuwng and xwaeH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary glosses the first 'harmonious' and 'bright' and, after an older book, as steam rising from a cooking pot; the second it glosses 'to change, to transform'. How the pair came to mean melting is usually explained as warmth changing a thing, but it is not settled."
            }
          ]
